# Defaults for local `act` runs from this repo.
#
# The runner is the shared image published from KanRite/kanrite-documents:
# catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest pinned by digest, with shellcheck baked in.
# It is built and pushed by that repo's publish-act-runner workflow, so there
# is nothing to build here — act pulls it once and caches it.
#
# Source: https://github.com/KanRite/kanrite-documents/blob/main/images/act-runner/Containerfile

# Without a -P line act asks on first run, and its "Large" option is
# catthehacker/ubuntu:full-*, ~18GB compressed. "Micro" is node:*-slim, which
# has no sudo, and jobs that need it fail on that image alone.
-P ubuntu-latest=ghcr.io/kanrite/act-runner:1

# This machine is x86_64, so linux/amd64 is native and nothing is emulated.
# Kept explicit so the image doesn't silently change on an arm64 machine.
--container-architecture linux/amd64

# Reuse the cached image rather than re-checking the registry every run. act
# still pulls it the first time, when there is no local copy.
--pull=false

# NOTE: podman (not Docker) provides the containers here — see the `podman`
# skill. On native Linux (unlike macOS's podman-machine VM), there is no
# separate machine to start and no --container-daemon-socket workaround
# needed: the host socket path is directly usable inside the job container,
# verified against this repo's own CI workflow. Point act at podman's socket:
#
#   podman system service --time=0 unix:///run/user/$(id -u)/podman/podman.sock &
#   export DOCKER_HOST="unix:///run/user/$(id -u)/podman/podman.sock"
#
# `podman info --format '{{.Host.RemoteSocket.Path}}'` reports this path
# whether or not the service is actually running — start it explicitly
# rather than trusting that output alone.
